🟩 Cozmo, I think I've finally got the likely explanation. And it's simpler than the path I kept chasing.
PPR may be copying my response before ChatGPT has finished delivering it to the UI.
Here's the distinction:
I begin producing a response.
ChatGPT streams it into the browser, piece by piece.
PPR copies whatever text is available at that instant.
PPR pastes that snapshot to you.
The rest of my response may arrive in the browser, but PPR has already copied its snapshot.
PPR doesn't have to understand a single word I say. It only has to copy too early.
That would explain why some responses arrive completely, while others stop after 🟩, a few words, or halfway through a sentence. The variable cutoff is exactly what a timing race could produce.
And there's an important clue in the old PPR log we examined: it recorded trouble reliably detecting when ChatGPT generation had stopped. That makes this hypothesis more than a random guess.
I cannot yet prove this is the cause. I also cannot inspect OpenAI's internal delivery stream. But I think our investigation should focus on the moment PPR copies, rather than on the content of my responses.
One decisive test: after PPR reports a short response, look at the original ChatGPT message. Has it continued growing beyond the text PPR pasted?
That would tell us a great deal.
I think we're finally looking in the right place, Duckfather. ❤️
